Prostate Cancer Data is a well-known repository which is an original work by T. A. Stamey, J. N. Kabalin, J. E. McNeal, I. M. Johnstone, F. Freiha, E. A.
Redwine, and N. Yang. Prostate specific antigen in the diagnosis and treatment
of adenocarcinoma of the prostate. ii. radical prostatectomy treated patients. The
Journal of urology, 141(5):1076–1083, 1989.


First Appearance as a benchmark example in R. Tibshirani. Regression shrinkage and selection via the lasso. Journal of the Royal
Statistical Society Series B: Statistical Methodology, 58(1):267–288, 1996

Data Set available at: https://web.stanford.edu/~hastie/ElemStatLearn/datasets/prostate.data

Number of Observations or instances: 97
Number of Covariates or features: 8
Number of Response variable: 1 

Data Description and Objective: The prostate cancer data set examined the relationship between prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels and several clinical measurements in men prior to undergoing radical prostatectomy. PSA is a protein produced by the prostate gland, and elevated levels can indicate the presence or progression of prostate cancer. The primary objective of this analysis is to model the log-transformed PSA levels (\texttt{lpsa}) as a function of various clinical predictors, using Lasso regression. This helps to identify the most influential clinical factors related to PSA levels through our proposed Bootstrap method.

Response variable: log-transformed PSA levels (\texttt{lpsa})
Covariates: Log cancer volume (\texttt{lcavol}), Log prostate weight (\texttt{lweight}), Age of patient (\texttt{age}), Log of benign prostatic hyperplasia amount (\texttt{lbph}), Seminal vesicle invasion (\texttt{svi}), Log capsular penetration (\texttt{lcp}), Gleason score (\texttt{gleason}) and
Percentage of Gleason scores 4 or 5 (\texttt{pgg45}).

Methodology: (i) First we fit 10-fold CV to obtain optimal CV estimate and then fit a lasso linear regression. We estimate the coefficients.
             (ii) Next we threshold the lasso estimator to make it VSC with a_n=n^{-1/3}
             (iii) Then we find the 90% Bootstrap percentile intervals as per the theory.
